\

::: exercise
<h2><i class="fas fa-exclamation"></i> Warning</h2>

Whenever you remove or overwrite files from the command line, they are gone. Forever. There is no Trash or Recycle Bin like within a graphical environment. There is no easy way to get them back!\
Some servers will have daily backups, however this is not something to depend on. Make sure you have backups of important data.\

One tip would be to always do an `ls` of the folder you want to remove before actually deleting it. This way you can make sure you are removing the right folder and are aware of what is inside the folder. You can also use the `-i` interactive option, this will ask you to confirm everything it removes. Be very aware of spaces in your command.

``` bash
rm -r ~/Project /temp2
```

Instead of removing the `~/Project/temp2` folder, the command above removes the full `Project` folder and the root `temp2` folder, because the space in the directory name. This removes much more than we would want.
:::

