To change permissions of files, the command `chmod` (change mode) can be used. 

- The `chmod` command uses the letters `u`, `g` and `o` to represent user, group and others.
- To change permissions for everyone you can use `a` for all.
- The `chmod` command uses the letters `r`, `w` and `x` to represent read, write and execute. 

For example, to stop other users on the system from accessing `myfile.txt`, we can remove the permissions for group and others:

``` bash
chmod g-r myfile.txt

chmod o-r myfile.txt

ls -l myfile.txt
```

If we want to add permissions, use a `+` instead of a `-`.\

Setting permissions recursively (directory and all files inside of it) is possible, for this the `-R` option can be used. The command below is a very handy command. It means "make my current directory and everything inside of it readable and writable by me". Note the uppercase `X` properly sets the directory permissions, remembering that we want both **read** and **execute** set on directories. If we used a lowercase `x` it would also make all the files executable, which we probably don't wait. 

``` bash
chmod -R u+rwX .
```

File permissions can be confusing, here is a useful [tool](https://chmod-calculator.com/) to help.

## Symbolic links

Sometimes, we do not want to duplicate files but do want to easily access them within a directory. Symbolic links, also known as soft link or symlink, are pseudo-file that link to another file or directory (similar to a shortcut in Windows).
In bioinformatics, these links are often used to reduce duplication of raw data files or reference genomes. \

Symbolic links are created using the `ln` command with option `-s`. The `-r` option makes the command behave more nicely, similar to the `cp` or `mv` command. 

``` bash
ln -sr ~/Intro_to_Linux/genomes/human/hg38/genome.fa ./
```
:::: challenge
<h2><i class="fas fa-pencil-alt"></i> Challenge:</h2>

Check if the link is created and what the permissions are for the link.
<details>

<summary>Solution</summary>

::: solution
<h2><i class="far fa-eye"></i> Solution:</h2>

Using `ls` we can look at the files to check if the file is there and using `-l` we can see the permission details.

``` bash
ls -l
```
:::
</details>
::::
\

Deleting symbolic links does not remove the original file. If, however, the target file to which the link is point is moved or deleted, the link will stop working. 

